% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Abstract -- SECTION-H: maximum 300 words; states the problem, the method, the results and
% the conclusions. Keywords: maximum five.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\frontsection{Abstract}

{\singlespacing
Cross-lingual speech synthesis can now clone a speaker's voice into a language that speaker never
used, but whether it also carries their prosody --- the pitch, rhythm and timing that make speech
expressive --- has rarely been measured, because most corpora contain no recording of what the same
speaker actually sounds like in the target language. This study implements a cascaded
English--Spanish speech-to-speech translation system (Whisper, MarianMT, XTTS-v2) and evaluates it
on the Dialogs Re-enacted Across Languages corpus, in which one bilingual speaker performs matched
content in both languages and therefore supplies a human cross-language reference for every
generated utterance. Eight hundred and seventy held-out utterances were synthesised in both
directions over a speaker-disjoint split, and speaker identity, intelligibility, translation
quality and prosody were measured against that reference.

Speaker identity reached 94.5\,\% (English to Spanish) and 86.1\,\% (Spanish to English) of the
human cross-language ceiling, so most of the identity that is retainable across a language switch
survives. Prosody did not follow. Within a speaker, human pitch level transfers at $r=0.787$,
whereas synthesised speech reaches only $0.311$ and $0.314$, and pitch range collapses from $0.214$
to $0.035$--$0.040$. Duration transfers better ($0.352$--$0.610$ against $0.870$), but raw synthesis
runs $1.52\times$ longer than the human target, which a WORLD-based retiming stage corrects inside
the accompanying dubbing application. A ridge model predicting target-language prosody from
source-language prosody improved on a copy-plus-offset baseline only for pitch range, by 9.5\,\% and
14.7\,\% mean absolute error, and no prosody result survives family-wise correction. Fine-tuning
MarianMT on the corpus raised BLEU by 2.34 and 3.23, which is register adaptation rather than
improved adequacy. The contribution is a calibrated measurement: zero-shot voice cloning preserves
identity but not prosody, and the size of that gap is now quantified against a human ceiling.
\par}

\vspace{1.0\baselineskip}
\noindent\textbf{\textit{Keywords:}} \textit{speech-to-speech translation; speaker identity;
cross-lingual prosody; zero-shot voice cloning; automatic dubbing}
